<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heidelberg University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1386">1386</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as University of Heidelberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> University was founded in 1386 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupert-i-elector-palatine">Rupert I, Elector Palatine</a>, and is the oldest university in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It was a centre of humanism and, after the Electoral Palatinate became Calvinist, of Reformed theology, associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> of 1563. When Heidelberg passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, Elector Charles Frederick reorganised the university in 1803, and it is named after him and Rupert.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university.jpg" alt="Heidelberg University" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uni Heidelberg; derivative work by Tou-x · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_University_of_Heidelberg.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Heidelberg University was founded in 1386 on the instruction of Pope Urban VI and was the third university established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> (1347) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> (1365). The Great Schism of 1378, which produced rival popes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, made it possible for Heidelberg, a relatively small city and capital of the Electorate of the Palatinate, to gain a university of its own. German rulers and churchmen supported the pope in Rome, and the German students and teachers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> lost their stipends and had to leave.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Between 1414 and 1418 professors of theology and law took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a> as counsellors to Louis III. It first taught the nominalism known as the via moderna. In 1412 realism and the teachings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-wycliffe">John Wycliffe</a> were forbidden, but around 1454 the university decided that realism would also be taught, so that two ways were taught in parallel.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>'s disputation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> in April 1518 made a deep impression, and his followers among the masters and scholars became leading Reformationists in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. When the Electorate of the Palatinate turned to the Reformed faith, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-henry-elector-palatine">Otto Henry, Elector Palatine</a>, made the university a Calvinist institution, and members of its divinity school took part in creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> in 1563. In 1803 Karl Friedrich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, to whom the part of the Palatinate on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> had been allotted, reestablished it as a state institution. He divided it into five faculties, and it has borne his name together with that of Ruprecht I ever since.</p><p>After the Nazi seizure of power in 1933 the university supported the regime like other German universities. It dismissed many staff and students for political and racial reasons, and members of the faculty and students took part in book burnings on 17 May 1933. The inscription over the New University, The Living Spirit, was changed to The German Spirit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Women have been admitted since 1899. Modern scientific psychiatry, psychopharmacology, experimental psychology, mathematical statistics and modern sociology were introduced as disciplines by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> students or faculty. Among its historical scientific achievements are the invention of spectroscopy and of the Bunsen burner and the discovery of the elements caesium and rubidium.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heidelberg University” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>University of Heidelberg</b> is the oldest university in present-day Germany.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A marginal note of October 1503 in a 1477 printing of Cicero, found in 2005 by a scholar at <b>Heidelberg University</b>, likens Leonardo to the Greek painter Apelles and says he was then working on a portrait of Lisa del Giocondo.</blockquote><a href="/work/mona-lisa">Mona Lisa · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A day after a Pontifical High Mass established it, <b>Heidelberg University</b>, founded by Rupert I, Elector Palatine, held its first lecture, making it the oldest university in present-day Germany.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-19">19 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>As elector he integrated the new lands, whose Catholic and Protestant populations had to be combined, with a new administration and courts, and the <b>University of Heidelberg</b> was reorganised in 1803.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-baden">Electorate of Baden</a></li></ul></section>
